\begin{remark}[Monotonicity]
$\xi(d)$ is strictly monotonically decreasing on $d\in\{1,\ldots,12\}$ because $A_{0}^{\text{magic}}(d) = (d-1)^{2}+16$ is strictly increasing on the non-negative integers. Lower-$d$ families couple more strongly; higher-$d$ families access finer structural detail but at correspondingly lower per-family coupling.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Harmonic families: the per-axis structural-mode layer}\label{subsec:harmonic-families}

The classification supplied by Proposition~\ref{prop:divisors} (\emph{sublattice family}, $d = N/\gcd(|k|,N)$) is one of two structurally distinct family-layers that the lattice carries on each axis. The sublattice-family layer is static: it classifies an individual lattice coordinate $k$ by the divisibility structure of $k$ relative to $N$, and the count of sublattice families at resolution $N$ is $\tau(N)$ --- six at $N=12$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:six}). The second layer is the per-axis enumeration of \emph{structural modes} --- gravity, weak, strong, electromagnetic, quintic-golden, septic-$G_{2}$, and so on --- each carrying a distinct physical character on its axis. The two layers are categorically distinct concepts that share a label space; they are not synonyms.

\begin{definition}[Harmonic family]\label{def:harmonic-family}
A \emph{harmonic family} on a single axis of $\mathcal{L}_{\mathbb{C}}$ is a structural mode of that axis labeled by an integer $d$ in the simple range $d \in \{1,2,\ldots,12\}$. There are twelve harmonic families per axis. The label $d$ identifies which sublattice family the harmonic family inhabits when native at the current resolution $N$, that is, when $d \mid N$; when $d \nmid N$ the harmonic family is present at the current resolution as a \emph{shadow} contribution (Definition~\ref{def:shadow-force}), with native lattice expression at the canonical resolution $n_{c}(d) = \mathrm{lcm}(N,d)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Discovery and verification via the palindromic cascade]\label{rem:cascade-discovery}
The harmonic-family layer is not posited externally --- it is discovered, and verified, by the canonical unit-generator cascade of Section~\ref{sec:cascade}. The cascade traverses the axis through $N$ cascade-position events (twelve events at base $N=12$ with generator $g=7$; see Definition~\ref{def:palindrome}), and each event is a realization of one of the harmonic families: the event at cascade index $n$ inhabits the harmonic family whose label is $d_{n} = N/\gcd(|k_{n}|,N)$, simultaneously fixing the harmonic-family label and its current sublattice-family inhabitation. The Sublattice Visitation Theorem of \S\ref{subsec:visitation} formalizes the multiplicity of cascade-position events per harmonic family. The palindromic cascade therefore plays a dual role: it is the \emph{discovery engine} for the per-axis harmonic-family structure (the sequence of cascade-position events makes the twelve harmonic families explicit, six visited natively at base $N=12$ plus six shadow), and the \emph{verification engine} for that structure (the palindromic symmetry and the totient multiplicities certify the count of twelve, with no count escaping or being double-counted).
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Sublattice family vs harmonic family --- categorical distinction]\label{rem:hf-vs-sf}
The two family-layers are distinct structural concepts, related but not interchangeable.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item A \emph{sublattice family} is a property of a single lattice coordinate $k$: $d = N/\gcd(|k|,N)$. It is the static gcd-classification of that coordinate. The count at resolution $N$ is $\tau(N)$ --- exactly six at $N=12$ (the divisors of $12$). All six are realized natively on each axis at $N=12$. Sublattice families are about the lattice itself.
\item A \emph{harmonic family} is a per-axis structural mode labeled by $d \in \{1,\ldots,12\}$. The count is twelve per axis at the simple-$d$ range. Six are simple ($d \mid 12$) --- corresponding to the six native sublattice families on the axis --- and six are complex ($d \nmid 12$) --- present at base $N=12$ as shadow contributions, native at higher LCM-tower resolutions. Harmonic families are about the cascade traversing the axis and the structural modes that traversal reveals.
\end{itemize}
At $N=12$ the six \emph{simple} harmonic families are gravity ($d=1$), tritone-pivot ($d=2$), strong/cubic ($d=3$), weak/quartic ($d=4$), hexadic/EW-composite ($d=6$), and EM/full-resolution ($d=12$); the six \emph{complex} harmonic families are quintic/golden ($d=5$), septic/$G_{2}$ ($d=7$), gluon-octet/SU($3$) ($d=8$), nonic/quark-$3{\times}3$ ($d=9$), decic/superstring ($d=10$), and undecimal/M-theory ($d=11$). The two-axis enumeration ($24$ total: $12$ on the real axis and $12$ on the imaginary axis) populates the Force Quadrant Grid of \S\ref{sec:catalog}; the connection is made explicit in Remark~\ref{rem:24-is-hf} of \S\ref{subsec:24-families}.
\end{remark}

