\subsection{The Sublattice Visitation Theorem}\label{subsec:visitation}

The connection between the sublattice-family layer and the harmonic-family layer is exact, and is the harmonic-family-layer reading of Theorem~\ref{thm:general-cascade}\,(2).

\begin{theorem}[Sublattice Visitation Theorem]\label{thm:visitation}
At resolution $N$, with any unit-residue generator $g \in (\Z/N\Z)^{*}$, the canonical cascade $r_{n} = (g\cdot n) \bmod N$ has exactly $\varphi(d)$ cascade-position events realizing harmonic family $d$, for each simple harmonic family ($d \mid N$). Equivalently:
\[
\bigl|\{\, n \in \{1,\ldots,N\} : d_{n} = d \,\}\bigr| \;=\; \varphi(d), \qquad \sum_{d\mid N} \varphi(d) \;=\; N.
\]
At base $N=12$ the multiplicities across the six simple harmonic families are
\[
\bigl(\varphi(1),\varphi(2),\varphi(3),\varphi(4),\varphi(6),\varphi(12)\bigr) \;=\; (1,1,2,2,2,4),
\]
summing to $12$ cascade-position events.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The statement is Theorem~\ref{thm:general-cascade}\,(2) re-expressed in harmonic-family language: by part~(5) of that theorem every unit generator yields the same divisor sequence; by part~(2) the multiplicities are $\varphi(d)$ for each $d \mid N$; the totient-sum identity $\sum_{d \mid N}\varphi(d) = N$ is Corollary~\ref{cor:totientpartition}. \qed
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Six simple plus six complex equals twelve harmonic families per axis]\label{cor:six-plus-six}
At $N=12$ the cascade visits the six simple harmonic families $d \in \{1,2,3,4,6,12\}$ with totient multiplicities $\{1,1,2,2,2,4\}$ summing to $12$ cascade-position events. The six complex harmonic families $d \in \{5,7,8,9,10,11\}$ are not visited as native lattice positions at base $N=12$; they are present as shadow harmonic families (Definition~\ref{def:shadow-force} below), and become natively visited at the higher LCM-tower resolution $n_{c}(d) = \mathrm{lcm}(12,d)$. The two sets together comprise the twelve harmonic families on each axis (Definition~\ref{def:harmonic-family}, Table~\ref{tab:twelve-harmonic-families}); the cascade is the discovery and verification engine that makes the count of twelve explicit.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[The two enumerations of the same twelve]\label{rem:two-enumerations}
The twelve harmonic families per axis admit two complementary enumerations: as the twelve cascade-position events at base $N=12$ traversed by the canonical unit-generator cascade (the discovery view), and as the twelve $d$-axis structural modes labeled by $d \in \{1,\ldots,12\}$ (the result view, with six SIMPLE plus six COMPLEX as in Definition~\ref{def:harmonic-family}). The two are not separate enumerations of separate objects: they are two angles on the same twelve-fold per-axis structure. The cascade view counts cascade-position events; the $d$-axis view counts structural modes, with shadow modes included. The Visitation Theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:visitation}) is the bridge: at base $N=12$, the cascade events distribute across the six simple modes with totient multiplicities $\{1,1,2,2,2,4\}$ (sum $12$), while the six complex modes are present as shadow contributions awaiting native realization at higher resolutions.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Shadow forces: sublattice families excluded at base $N=12$}\label{subsec:shadow-forces}

The base resolution $N=12$ supports the six divisor families $d\in\{1,2,3,4,6,12\}$. Six further simple sublattice families $d\in\{5,7,8,9,10,11\}$ are non-divisors of $12$ and are excluded at base resolution. They become native at the lattice resolution $\mathrm{lcm}(12,d)$ and at every higher multiple. Their structural status as ``shadow forces'' at base is the lattice expression of the empirical observation that the corresponding physical structures are present in nature but require structural detail beyond what $N=12$ alone can resolve.

\begin{definition}[Shadow force]\label{def:shadow-force}
A \emph{shadow force} (or \emph{shadow family}) at base resolution $N=12$ is a sublattice family $d \in \{5,7,8,9,10,11\}$ whose structural content is excluded at $N=12$ ($d \nmid 12$) and appears as a native family at the canonical higher resolution $\mathrm{lcm}(12,d)$. The minimal-host resolution and physical identification are tabulated in Table~\ref{tab:shadow-forces}.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{c|c|l|l}
\toprule
$d$ & $N_{\text{native}} = \mathrm{lcm}(12,d)$ & Symmetry character & Identified physical sector \\ \midrule
$8$  & $24$  & Octet (linear) & SU(3) gauge octet (gluon) \\
$9$  & $36$  & Nonic & Quark colour $\times$ generation grid \\
$5$  & $60$  & Quintic / pentagonal & Golden / qualia / icosahedral \\
$10$ & $60$  & Decic & 10D superstring transverse spectrum \\
$7$  & $84$  & Septic / G$_{2}$ & Octonion-automorphism / G$_{2}$ structures \\
$11$ & $132$ & Undecimal & 11D M-theory transverse spectrum \\ \bottomrule
