The fractal is therefore not a cosmetic visualisation of the lattice but the lattice's natural dynamical instantiation: the fixed-exponent families of standard complex dynamics correspond to single-family sublattice projections $d\in\{1,\ldots,12\}$, and the $\partial I$ system is the unique complex dynamical system that simultaneously honours all twelve families. In this sense Mandelbrot and quartic Multibrot and linear dynamics are \emph{restrictions} of the lattice-aware system to a single sublattice, and the $\partial I$ fractal is the unrestricted image.

\begin{remark}[$\partial I$ dynamics in optical singularity ensembles]\label{rem:dI-optics}
The $\partial I$ boundary dynamics are verified empirically in the optical phase singularity data of Bucher et al.~\cite{Bucher2025}. Phase singularities in phonon-polariton fields satisfy six structural properties of $\partial I$ configurations: zero $D$-content (amplitude) at the core; asymptotic approach without penetration; the $\{P,T\}$ manifold state (Incoherence) at the singularity; marginal stability under perturbation; open-set topology; and the Mediation Problem (Theorem~\ref{thm:mediation-problem}) --- the phase winding ($T$) exists in the neighbourhood, not at the core, because $D$ is absent. The measured sub-cycle creation and annihilation dynamics ($3$\,fs $\approx \tau/8$) are $\partial I$ boundary transitions observed at deep sub-wavelength, deep sub-cycle resolution. One polariton oscillation cycle corresponds to one cascade step on the $T$-axis; the sub-cycle temporal resolution ($< 1$ step) resolves the internal structure of a single $T$-cascade level, confirming that $T$-content topological transitions (singularity creation and annihilation) resolve within the $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ coherence window of Proposition~\ref{prop:stability}.
\end{remark}

% ===========================================================
\section{Three minimal backbones at $N=12$}\label{sec:backbones}
% ===========================================================

So far the projection formula has been treated as a black box. This section opens it, identifies its internal PDT structure, and shows that $N=12$ is simultaneously the natural scale of three independently-derived minimal-generator objects --- one on each of the three categories discrete-logical, discrete-multiplicative, continuous-elementary.

\subsection{PDT decomposition of the projection formula}\label{subsec:pdt-decomp}

The universal projection formula $(k,d,\varepsilon) = \Pi_{N}(r)$ of \S\ref{sec:lattice} decomposes into six atomic operations whose PDT roles are forced by what each does:

\begin{theorem}[PDT decomposition of the projection]\label{thm:pdt-decomp}
The projection $\Pi_{N}:\R^{+}\to\Z\times\{N/d : d\mid N\}\times\R$ factors as
\[
\Pi_{N} \;=\; \underbrace{\bigl(\log_{2},\, N\cdot,\, \text{gap computation}\bigr)}_{\text{continuous $D$}} \;\circ\; \underbrace{\round}_{T\text{-act}} \;\circ\; \underbrace{\bigl(\gcd,\, N/g\bigr)}_{\text{discrete $D$}},
\]
in which the continuous-$D$ operations are implementable as finite compositions of a single binary operator, the rounding is the only non-reversible step (hence the only $T$-act), and the discrete-$D$ operations are Euclidean-algorithm arithmetic.
\end{theorem}

The operation that implements the continuous-$D$ part is the EML operator.

\subsection{EML: the continuous-$D$ minimal generator (Odrzywołek 2026)}\label{subsec:eml}

\begin{definition}[The EML operator~\cite{Odrzywolek2026}]\label{def:eml}
The EML operator is the binary function
\[
\operatorname{eml}(x,y) \;=\; \exp(x)\,-\,\ln(y),
\]
understood as defined on the complex domain with the principal branch of $\ln$. The EML grammar is the single-terminal inductive specification $S\to 1 \mid \operatorname{eml}(S,S)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[EML completeness, Odrzywołek 2026]\label{thm:eml-complete}
Every function of the scientific-calculator basis --- the constants $\pi,e,i,-1,0,1,2$, the $20$ standard unary elementary functions $(\exp,\ln,\sin,\cos,\tan$, their hyperbolic and inverse counterparts, $\sec,\csc,\cot,\operatorname{sech},\operatorname{csch},\operatorname{coth})$, and the $8$ standard binary operations $(+,-,\times,/,\,\wedge,\bmod,\log,\operatorname{comb})$ --- is expressible as a finite EML tree over the single terminal constant $1$.
\end{theorem}

The proof is the constructive enumeration of finite trees in \S3 of~\cite{Odrzywolek2026}; key instances include $e = \operatorname{eml}(1,1)$ (since $\exp(1)-\ln(1)=e$), $\ln z = \operatorname{eml}(1,\operatorname{eml}(\operatorname{eml}(1,z),1))$, and $xy = \exp(\ln x + \ln y)$ implemented as a depth-$8$ EML tree.

\begin{theorem}[EML minimality under the Subsumption Law]\label{thm:eml-minimal}
In the category of continuous-$D$ generators on the multiplicative manifold, EML is minimal: (i) EML cannot be written as a reduction of any simpler binary operator together with unary post-processing; (ii) no alternative proposed continuous-$D$ generator subsumes EML without increasing operator count or terminal count; (iii) EML subsumes every standard elementary function without remainder (Theorem~\ref{thm:eml-complete}).
\end{theorem}

Conditions (i)--(iii) are the three Subsumption Law conditions of \S\ref{subsec:subsumption}. The minimality argument of~\cite{Odrzywolek2026} \S5 establishes (i) directly: \emph{``no further reduction of operator count is possible, because at least one binary operator and at least one terminal symbol are required''}. (ii) is the absence of a proposed subsumer in the literature after the 2026 paper's exhaustive search. (iii) is Theorem~\ref{thm:eml-complete}.

\begin{corollary}[EML is ET-native]
EML is not an external tool borrowed into the Sempaevum framework. It is the structural identity of the continuous-$D$ category at its Subsumption-minimum. Its discovery in 2026 by independent symbolic-regression search confirms, rather than imposes, a structural fact that the ET framework requires.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Three continuous Sheffer variants]\label{rem:three-sheffers}
Equations (4a)--(4c) of~\cite{Odrzywolek2026} yield three continuous Sheffer operators: $\operatorname{eml}(x,y) = \exp(x)-\ln(y)$ with constant~$1$, $\operatorname{edl}(x,y) = \exp(x)/\ln(y)$ with constant~$e$, and $-\!\operatorname{eml}(y,x) = \ln(x)-\exp(y)$ with constant~$-\infty$. Each generates the full elementary-function basis from a single binary operator paired with a single distinguished constant. The three constants correspond to the three primitive anchors: $1 = P$ (the multiplicative identity), $e = D$ (the natural base of $\exp$), and $-\infty = T$ (the $\partial I$ boundary, since $\ln 0 = -\infty$). Three variants generating the same totality from three primitive-centred perspectives is $3=3=3=\Sigma$ at the continuous-mathematics level.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}[No constant-free continuous Sheffer]\label{cor:no-constantfree}
A continuous Sheffer operator without a distinguished constant would instantiate the configuration $\{D,T\}$ (Mediation, Definition~\ref{def:states}) --- a binary operator without a substrate anchor. By the Identification Principle (Principle~\ref{princ:identification}), $\{D,T\}$ is structurally incomplete: the Mediation state is one of the three non-Exception configurations. The constant~$1$ in EML is the $P$-element that grounds the composition; $\ln 1 = 0$ neutralises the $T$-component so that $\operatorname{eml}(x,1) = \exp(x)$ reaches the $D$-axis alone. This resolves the open question of~\cite{Odrzywolek2026}~\S5 in the negative.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[Complex domain as two-axis necessity]\label{rem:complex-domain}
The observation of~\cite{Odrzywolek2026}~\S5 that ``a continuous Sheffer working purely in the real domain seems impossible'' is the statement that $(\R^{+},\times)$ alone --- the $D$-axis of $\C^{\times}$ --- cannot generate the full elementary-function basis: phase content, rotation, and periodicity require the $T$-axis $(U(1),\times)$. The complex domain requirement is the two-axis structure of the lattice $\mathcal{L}_{\C}$ at the continuous-mathematics level.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[EML depth--coherence correspondence]\label{rem:eml-depth}
The gradient-based recovery experiments of~\cite{Odrzywolek2026} \S4.3 report the following blind recovery rates for elementary-function identities from random initialisation (over $1000+$ runs):
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}r r r@{}}
\toprule
EML depth & Blind recovery & Accumulated $|\delta_{\theta}|$\\
\midrule
$2$ & $100\%$ & $0.447$ ($< 0.5$, coherent)\\
$3$ & $\sim 25\%$ & $0.670$ ($> 0.5$, ambiguous)\\
$5$ & $<1\%$ & $1.117$\\
$6$ & $0\%$ ($448$ attempts) & $1.340$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The transition from $100\%$ to $\sim 25\%$ at depth $2 \to 3$ coincides with the imaginary-axis cascade stability limit $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:stability}: each EML tree level is a $T$-step on $\C^{\times}$, accumulating $|\delta_{\theta}|$ per level; the accumulated residual crosses the $0.5$ rounding threshold at level~$3$. Correct basins of attraction exist at all depths --- recovery from perturbed weights succeeds at $100\%$ even at depth~$6$ --- but their discovery from random initialisation requires the accumulated residual to remain within the coherence window $n \le n_{\max,\theta}=2$, exactly as the cascade predicts.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Cross-domain appearance of $n_{\max,\theta}=2$}\label{subsec:cross-domain-nmax}

The imaginary-axis cascade stability limit $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:stability}) appears independently in three domains that do not reference one another. This subsection synthesises the cross-domain link.

\subsubsection*{The structural constant}

The accumulated $T$-residual after $n$ cascade steps is $n\,|\delta_{\theta}|$, with coherence requiring $n\,|\delta_{\theta}| < 0.5$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}r r l@{}}
\toprule
Steps $n$ & Accumulated $|\delta_{\theta}|$ & Status\\
\midrule
$1$ & $0.2234$ & $< 0.5$ --- coherent\\
$\mathbf{2}$ & $\mathbf{0.4467}$ & $\mathbf{< 0.5}$ \textbf{--- coherent (last coherent step)}\\
$3$ & $0.6701$ & $> 0.5$ --- ambiguous rounding\\
$4$ & $0.8934$ & $> 0.5$ --- deep ambiguity\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
No parameter is chosen; the limit is forced by $|\delta_{\theta}| = |24\pi/\ln 2 - 109| = 0.22336\ldots$ and the transcendence of $\pi/\ln 2$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:cascadetheta}).

\subsubsection*{Domain 1: EML symbolic regression}

The EML operator $\operatorname{eml}(x,y) = \exp(x) - \ln(y)$ generates all elementary functions via binary trees of depth $1$--$8$~\cite{Odrzywolek2026}. Each tree level is one $T$-step: $T$ navigating the $D$-structured complex plane via recursive application of $\operatorname{eml}$, traversing the imaginary axis via $\ln(-1) = i\pi$. The gradient-based recovery experiments of Remark~\ref{rem:eml-depth} show $100\%$ blind recovery at depth~$\le 2$ and a sharp transition to $\sim 25\%$ at depth~$3$, with the accumulated $|\delta_{\theta}|$ crossing the $0.5$ threshold at exactly that boundary. The basins of attraction exist at all depths (recovery from perturbed weights succeeds at $100\%$ even at depth~$6$), but their discovery from random initialisation requires the accumulated residual to remain within the coherence window $n \le n_{\max,\theta}=2$.

\subsubsection*{Domain 2: optical phase singularities}

Bucher et al.~\cite{Bucher2025} measure two classes of statistical observable in ensembles of phonon-polariton singularities in hBN (Remark~\ref{rem:bucher-cascade}). Distance correlations are a $D$-content observable on the real axis, with $n_{\max,r}=25$ levels of cascade stability; they match the Gaussian random wave model precisely and exhibit particle-like liquid statistics. Velocity distributions are a $T$-content observable on the imaginary axis, with $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ levels; they exhibit a massive superluminal tail ($29\%$ of singularities exceed $c$, $\langle v\rangle \approx 1.04\,c$) that violates the Maxwell--J\"uttner distribution. The particle analogy holds for $D$-content ($25$ levels) and breaks for $T$-content ($2$ levels) --- the same structural constant governing the same axis.

\subsubsection*{Domain 3: the ET lattice}

$n_{\max,\theta}=2$ is not empirical. It is derived from the lattice's own geometry: $|\delta_{\theta}| = |24\pi/\ln 2 - 109|$ follows from the transcendence of $\pi/\ln 2$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:cascadetheta}), and the stability limit is $\lfloor 0.5/|\delta_{\theta}|\rfloor = 2$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:stability}). The number~$2$ appears because $2 \times 0.22336 = 0.4467 < 0.5$ while $3 \times 0.22336 = 0.6701 > 0.5$.

\subsubsection*{Synthesis}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l l@{}}
\toprule
Domain & What $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ governs & Data source\\
\midrule
ET lattice & Imaginary-axis cascade coherence depth & Mathematical derivation\\
EML trees~\cite{Odrzywolek2026} & Maximum depth for blind symbolic recovery & $1000+$ training experiments\\
Optical singularities~\cite{Bucher2025} & Transition from particle-like to non-particle statistics & $285$ frames $\times$ ${\sim}50$ singularities\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

These are three completely independent domains --- a mathematical structure, a computer-science optimisation problem, and an experimental physics measurement. None references the others. The structural constant $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ governs all three because each involves $T$-content traversals on the imaginary axis of $\mathcal{L}_{\C}$: the EML paper traverses the imaginary axis via $\ln(-1) = i\pi$ at each tree level; the Bucher paper measures $T$-content (phase/velocity) observables whose cascade depth is $n_{\max,\theta}=2$; the lattice analysis derives the limit from $|\delta_{\theta}| = |24\pi/\ln 2 - 109|$.

The cross-domain appearance of $n_{\max,\theta}=2$ is a falsifiable structural prediction: any system whose $T$-content involves recursive imaginary-axis traversals should exhibit a coherence transition at step~$2$.

\subsection{Webb 1935: the discrete-logical minimal generator at $n=12$}\label{subsec:webb}

\begin{definition}[The Webb stroke~\cite{Webb1935}]\label{def:webb}
For a positive integer $n\ge 2$, the \emph{Webb stroke} is the binary operation on $\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$ given by
\[
i \mathbin{|} j \;=\; \begin{cases}\, 0 & i \neq j \\ (i+1) \bmod n & i = j.\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Webb completeness]\label{thm:webb-complete}
Iteration of the Webb stroke on the $n$ constants $\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$ generates every function of every arity in the $n$-valued propositional logic on $\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}$.
\end{theorem}

The proof is Webb's constructive enumeration~\cite{Webb1935}, extending Sheffer 1913~\cite{Sheffer1913} from $n=2$ (the NAND/NOR case) to arbitrary $n$.

\begin{corollary}[Webb minimality at $n=12$]\label{cor:webb-minimal}
At $n=12$, the Webb stroke is the unique (up to relabelling) minimal generator of the $12$-valued propositional logical algebra. No single binary operation on $\{0,\ldots,11\}$ generates $12$-valued logic with a smaller algebraic presentation.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[PDT decomposition of the Webb stroke at $n=12$]\label{thm:webb-pdt}
The Webb stroke at $n=12$ decomposes into PDT components in direct analogy with the projection formula:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}XX@{}}
\toprule
Component of the Webb stroke & PDT role\\
\midrule
The $12$-element substrate $\{0,\ldots,11\}$ & $P$ (discrete $12$-point substrate, matching manifold symmetry)\\
The zero output for $i\ne j$ & $D$ (maximum-gap annihilation, analog of $r=0$ boundary)\\
The cyclic successor $(i+1)\bmod 12$ for $i=j$ & $T$ (single-step navigation on the $12$-cycle)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}
Thus the Webb stroke is itself a PDT-complete configuration at the discrete-logical scale.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The palindromic cascade: the discrete-multiplicative minimal generator on $\{d:d\mid 12\}$}\label{subsec:palindromic}

The palindromic cascade of \S\ref{sec:cascade} is the third minimal-generator object. Its defining sequence
\[
\text{PAL} \;=\; [12,\,6,\,4,\,3,\,12,\,2,\,12,\,3,\,4,\,6,\,12,\,1]
\]
visits every divisor of $12$ --- each of the six simple sublattice families of Proposition~\ref{prop:divisors} --- in a CPT-symmetric order (palindromic under the index reflection $n\mapsto 12-n$). It is the minimal discrete-multiplicative generator: every divisor of $12$ appears at least once; the sequence is its own inverse under reflection; no shorter CPT-symmetric sequence visits every divisor.

\begin{theorem}[Palindromic-cascade minimality]\label{thm:palindromic-minimal}
The palindromic cascade passes the three Subsumption Law conditions in the category of discrete-multiplicative generators on $\{d : d \mid 12\}$:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=*]
\item it cannot be reduced to a strictly shorter CPT-symmetric divisor-traversal;
\item no external CPT-symmetric traversal on $\{d:d\mid 12\}$ subsumes it;
\item it traverses every divisor of $12$ without remainder.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

The proof is the direct enumeration of divisors $\{1,2,3,4,6,12\}$ against the PAL sequence, together with a pigeonhole argument on shorter palindromes (no palindrome of length $<12$ visits all six values with the required reflection symmetry).

\subsection{The triple minimal-backbone theorem}\label{subsec:triple-backbone}

\begin{theorem}[Triple minimal backbone at $N=12$]\label{thm:triple-backbone}
Three independent minimal generators, one per structural category, are all native to the resolution $N=12$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}lXlX@{}}
\toprule
Category & Generator & Scale & Subsumption status\\
\midrule
Discrete-logical & Webb stroke $i\mathbin{|}j$ (1935) & $n=12$ & Minimal in $12$-valued logic (Cor.~\ref{cor:webb-minimal})\\
Discrete-multiplicative & Palindromic cascade & divisors of $12$ & Minimal CPT-symmetric traversal (Thm.~\ref{thm:palindromic-minimal})\\
Continuous-elementary & EML $\operatorname{eml}(x,y)$ (2026) & $(\R^{+},\times)$ with $N=12$ discretisation & Minimal under Subsumption (Thm.~\ref{thm:eml-minimal})\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}
Three independent mathematical searches --- Sheffer 1913 and Webb 1935 in discrete logic, the ET-internal cascade derivation, Odrzywołek 2026 in symbolic regression over continuous mathematics --- each converge on minimal-generator objects whose natural scale is $N=12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The non-triviality of Theorem~\ref{thm:triple-backbone} is that three different minimum-complexity arguments, applied in three disjoint mathematical categories, yield the same integer $12$. The first two (Webb, palindromic) both descend from \emph{$N=12$ is already fixed}; the third (EML, 2026) was discovered independently by a process unaware of ET and is minimal under its own continuous-category Subsumption argument, then paired by Theorem~\ref{thm:pdt-decomp} with the $N=12$ discretisation that the projection formula imposes. No external adjustment aligns the three scales; they coincide.
\end{remark}

% ===========================================================
\section{The Four Projection Paths and essential-infinity handling}\label{sec:paths}
% ===========================================================

The projection formula of \S\ref{sec:lattice} takes a positive real $r$ as input. For a large collection of mathematical objects --- formal systems, indeterminate forms, non-computable reals, objects with genuinely infinite structure --- there is no canonical single $r$. This section partitions all user-input forms into four paths A, B, C, D, verifies that the four subsume every possible input, and in particular shows that Path D handles essential-infinity objects without invoking limits.

\subsection{The four paths}

\begin{definition}[The Four Projection Paths]\label{def:four-paths}
Every input to the projection apparatus falls into exactly one of:
\begin{description}[leftmargin=*,style=nextline]
\item[Path A --- Direct projection (canonical terminal).] A positive real $r$ is supplied directly. Apply $\Pi_{N}$ of \S\ref{sec:lattice}.
\item[Path B --- Limit convergence (computational technique).] A convergent sequence $\{s_{k}\}\to r$ is supplied. Compute $s_{k}$ to sufficient precision, then apply Path A to the resulting $r$. This is a \emph{technique}, not a separate ontological path.
\item[Path C --- Meta-descriptor extraction (alternative method).] A structural object (a graph, a formal system, an encoding scheme) with no canonical $r$ is supplied together with a user-chosen descriptor ratio $r = Q/R_{0}$ constructed from the object's finite $D$-content. Apply Path A to this chosen ratio. The projection is of the chosen ratio, not of the object's essential character; the user bears the choice of ratio.
\item[Path D --- Primitive-native infinity handling (no limits).] The input's essential character is infinity-valued, where ``infinity'' is intrinsic and cannot be reduced to a single finite $r$ without information loss. Four sub-paths, one per primitive face of infinity:
\begin{description}
\item[D.P:] continuous, uncountable, or non-computable positive reals (e.g., Chaitin's $\Omega$, generic reals, specific irrationals of unknown computability).
\item[D.D:] unbound descriptor-constraint structures (e.g., non-divisor-of-$12$ sublattice families $d\in\{5,7,8,9,10,11\}$ and composites up to $d=132$, infinite axiom totalities).
\item[D.T:] indeterminate forms $\{0/0,\infty/\infty,0\cdot\infty,0^{0},1^{\infty},\infty^{0},\infty-\infty\}$, oscillatory divergent limits, measurement operators.
\item[D.PDT:] integrated off-axis Exception objects with both magnitude and phase character (e.g., most physical particles, complex-valued observables).
\end{description}
\end{description}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Path D requires no limits]\label{thm:d-no-limits}
Each sub-path of Path D terminates via finite operations on finite quantities at a specific resolution $N$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}lXX@{}}
\toprule
Sub-path & Operation & Limit invoked?\\
\midrule
D.P & Read the exact log-position $N\log_{2}r$ and round & No --- $\log_{2}$, round, and $\R^{+}$ are defined directly\\
D.D & Identify the $(d_{r},d_{\theta})$ shadow cell at $12$ET via the shadow diagnostic & No --- each step is finite arithmetic at a specific $N$\\
D.T & Place the $T$-signature at a structural lattice position (half-integer, annihilation boundary) & No --- these positions are defined by the lattice structure directly\\
D.PDT & Apply the complex two-axis projection $(k_{r},k_{\theta},d_{r},d_{\theta},d_{\mathrm{comb}},\varepsilon_{r},\varepsilon_{\theta})$ & No --- finite arithmetic on both axes\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Path D is the critical upgrade that licenses the Sempaevum to host essential-infinity objects. The usual calculus of limits operates as follows: an infinite structure is approached by a sequence of finite approximations, and the object's properties are the limiting properties. Path D bypasses the approach entirely: each primitive (P, D, T) has its own structural handle on infinity --- $P$'s cardinality $\Omega$, $D$'s unbound constraint fields, $T$'s $[0/0]$ indeterminacy --- and the three primitives are already structurally present at \emph{every finite} lattice resolution. Infinity is handled \emph{on} the lattice, not via approach to it.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Subsumption of user inputs}

\begin{theorem}[Four-Path subsumption]\label{thm:four-path-subsumption}
For every input $X$ that any mathematical or physical object could present to the projection apparatus:
\[
X \;\in\; \mathrm{A} \;\cup\; \mathrm{B} \;\cup\; \mathrm{C} \;\cup\; \mathrm{D} \;\cup\; \text{Incoherence Filter},
\]
with $\mathrm{D} = \mathrm{D.P}\cup\mathrm{D.D}\cup\mathrm{D.T}\cup\mathrm{D.PDT}$. The union is disjoint up to the user's choice between A and C (when the object admits both) and A and B (when a convergent sequence terminates to an $r$ that can be supplied directly).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Case analysis on the essential character of $X$. If $X$ is a positive real directly specified, A. If $X$ is specified by a convergent sequence, B. If $X$ is a structural object for which the user explicitly chooses a descriptor ratio, C. If $X$'s essential character is infinite --- continuum, unbound-$D$, $[0/0]$, or off-axis PDT --- D.P, D.D, D.T, or D.PDT respectively. If $X$ is $\{P,T\}$ Incoherent (self-defeating), the Incoherence Filter assigns no lattice position; such configurations sit at $\partial I$ and are explicitly \emph{nowhere} on the lattice. Every mathematical or physical object describable at all falls into one of these cases. \qed
\end{proof}

Worked example --- Chaitin's $\Omega$: for the Calude--Dinneen universal Turing machine, $\Omega \approx 0.00787499699$ (first computed bits). Applying $\Pi_{12}$:
\[
\begin{aligned}
k \;&=\; \round(12\log_{2}0.00787499699) \;\approx\; -84,\\
d \;&=\; 12/\gcd(84,12) \;=\; 1,\\
\eps \;&\approx\; +13.794\ \text{cents}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Chaitin's $\Omega$ has lattice address $(-84, 1, +13.794$\,\textcent$)$ --- the $d=1$ octave / universal-period-closure sublattice. The $T$-cannot-produce-more-digits character is precisely the $\{P,D\}$ Unsubstantiated manifold state: the descriptor is finite (``halting probability of the universal Turing machine''), the position is determined, and no amount of computation will extend the bit-expansion further --- \emph{yet the address is a definite single point}.

% ===========================================================
\section{Mathematics as a domain of the Sempaevum}\label{sec:math-domain}
% ===========================================================

The Domain Validity Theorem (\S\ref{sec:primitives}) guarantees that any domain with an internally consistent $D$-set occupies valid lattice positions. Mathematics itself is such a domain --- arguably the most important one for a framework claiming $3=3=3=\Sigma$ universality. This section applies the Identification Principle to the domain of mathematics and projects its structural features onto the Sempaevum.

\subsection{The $P$, $D$, $T$ of mathematics}

\begin{definition}[Mathematics as a domain]\label{def:math-domain}
The mathematics domain is identified as:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Primitive & Identification\\
\midrule
$P_{\mathrm{math}}$ & the substrate of all possible consistent formal configurations\\
$D_{\mathrm{math}}$ & axioms, inference rules, definitions, formal languages\\
$T_{\mathrm{math}}$ & the mathematician or proof-assistant --- agency navigating $D_{\mathrm{math}}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The substrate-derived reference period is $R_{0}^{\mathrm{math}} = 1$ axiom (the minimal $T$-commitment a mathematician can make). This is not a unit choice; it is the smallest closed $T$-traversal loop the mathematical substrate supports.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Axiom-count projections of standard formal systems}

\begin{theorem}[Formal-system lattice addresses]\label{thm:axiom-addresses}
Projecting $r = (\text{axiom count})/R_{0}^{\mathrm{math}} = (\text{axiom count})/1$ at $12$ET yields the following lattice addresses for standard formal systems:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r r l@{}}
\toprule
Formal system & Axioms & $(k,d,\varepsilon)$ at $12$ET & Sublattice\\
\midrule
Propositional logic (Hilbert) & $3$ & $(+19, 12, +1.955$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=12$ Koide/triadic-binding\\
Equational group theory & $3$ & $(+19, 12, +1.955$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=12$ Koide/triadic-binding\\
Euclid's \emph{Elements} & $5$ & $(+28, 3, -13.686$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=3$ cubic\\
Robinson arithmetic & $7$ & $(+34, 6, -31.174$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=6$ hexadic\\
ZF set theory & $8 = 2^{3}$ & $(+36, 1, 0.000$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=1$ octave/gravitational \\
Peano arithmetic & $9$ & $(+38, 6, +3.910$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=6$ hexadic\\
ZFC (ZF + Choice) & $9$ & $(+38, 6, +3.910$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=6$ hexadic\\
Morse--Kelley class theory & $10$ & $(+40, 3, -13.686$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=3$ cubic\\
NBG (finitely axiomatised) & $18$ & $(+50, 6, +3.910$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=6$ hexadic\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

Three structural readings of Theorem~\ref{thm:axiom-addresses} are worth isolating.

\begin{corollary}[ZF is the tightest-bound formal system]\label{cor:zf-tight}
ZF with $8=2^{3}$ axioms lands at $d=1$ octave with $\varepsilon = 0$ exactly. ZF is therefore the unique formal system in Theorem~\ref{thm:axiom-addresses} that sits at a pure zero-descriptor-gap lattice point.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The axiom of Choice is a sublattice transition]\label{cor:ac-transition}
Adding Choice to ZF (moving from $8$ to $9$ axioms) carries the system from $(+36, 1, 0$\,\textcent$)$ to $(+38, 6, +3.910$\,\textcent$)$ --- a $d=1 \to d=6$ sublattice transition. The signature of the axiom of Choice is therefore structurally precise: Choice moves a formal system from the gravitational-class foundation to the composite hexadic class.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[The triadic-binding logical systems]\label{cor:triadic-logics}
Three-axiom foundational systems (Hilbert propositional logic, equational group theory) occupy the $d=12$ Koide attractor position $(+19, 12, +1.955$\,\textcent$)$. These are the systems whose axiom count matches the cardinality of the Cardinal triad $\{P,D,T\}$ itself.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Chaitin's $\Omega$ via Path D.P}

\begin{theorem}[Chaitin's $\Omega$ has a definite lattice address]\label{thm:chaitin}
For the Calude--Dinneen universal Turing machine $U$ with first-computed bits $\Omega_{U} \approx 0.00787499699$, the lattice address via Path D.P is
\[
(k, d, \varepsilon)_{\Omega_{U}}\Big|_{N=12} \;=\; (-84,\, 1,\, +13.794\ \text{cents}).
\]
$\Omega_{U}$ inhabits the $d=1$ octave sublattice --- the universal-period-closure family --- and exhibits the $\{P,D\}$ Unsubstantiated manifold state.
\end{theorem}

The non-computability of $\Omega_{U}$ is precisely the $\{P,D\}$-character: the descriptor is complete (the definition ``halting probability summed over all prefix-free programs''), the address is determined, but no $T$ (finite computational process) can produce further bits.

\subsection{G\"odel sentences via integrative-level classification}

\begin{theorem}[G\"odel sentences are integrative-level-dependent]\label{thm:godel}
For a formal system $\mathcal{S}$ admitting G\"odel's incompleteness theorem, the canonical G\"odel sentence $G_{\mathcal{S}}$ takes three distinct manifold classifications depending on the viewer's integrative level:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Viewer's system & Classification of $G_{\mathcal{S}}$\\
\midrule
$\mathcal{S}$ itself & $\{P,T\}$ Incoherence at $\partial I$ (the Incoherence Filter fires)\\
Stronger system $\mathcal{S}'\vdash\mathrm{Con}(\mathcal{S})$ & $\{P,D,T\}$ Exception (has a definite lattice address)\\
Outside any fixed system & $\{P,D\}$ Unsubstantiated (finite syntactic $D$, awaiting $T$)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{theorem}

The multi-address hosting at different integrative levels is the correct lattice classification of formally-undecidable propositions.

\subsection{Large cardinals via consistency-strength}

\begin{theorem}[Large-cardinal projections]\label{thm:large-cardinals}
Projecting large cardinals via their consistency-strength ordinal yields the following addresses at $12$ET:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l r r l@{}}
\toprule
Large cardinal & Consistency level & $(k,d,\varepsilon)$ at $12$ET & Sublattice\\
\midrule
Inaccessible & $1$ & $(0, 1, 0)$ & Unison\\
Mahlo & $2$ & $(+12, 1, 0)$ & Octave\\
Weakly compact & $3$ & $(+19, 12, +1.955$\,\textcent$)$ & Koide $d=12$\\
Measurable & $7$ & $(+34, 6, -31.174$\,\textcent$)$ & Hexadic\\
Supercompact & $\sim 15$ & $(+47, 12, -11.732$\,\textcent$)$ & $d=12$ EM\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Inaccessible and Mahlo cardinals sit at $d=1$ (the same gravitational sublattice as ZF). Measurable cardinals at $d=6$ share the hexadic sublattice with Robinson arithmetic, Peano arithmetic, ZFC, and NBG.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The universality statement for mathematics}

\begin{theorem}[Mathematical universality]\label{thm:math-universality}
Every mathematical object --- every number, function, formal-system statement, proof, undecidable proposition, large cardinal, impredicative definition --- has a lattice address:
\begin{multline*}
\forall X\ \text{(mathematical object)}: \quad X \in \Sigma \;\Rightarrow\; {} \\
\exists (k, d, \varepsilon) \in \Z\times\{N_{\ell}/d : d\mid N_{\ell}\}\times [-50, 50]\ \text{cents} \\
\text{at some tower resolution}\ N_{\ell}.
\end{multline*}
The address is explicit for directly-specified objects (Path A), accessible via convergence for limit-specified objects (Path B), parameterised by descriptor choice for structural objects (Path C), and well-defined without limits for essentially-infinite or non-computable objects (Path D).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$X \in \Sigma$ holds for any mathematical object $X$ by the universality anchor (Axiom~\ref{ax:anchor}): everything describable is in the totality. The Four-Path subsumption (Theorem~\ref{thm:four-path-subsumption}) partitions the describable space into the four paths (plus the Incoherence Filter, which itself \emph{is} a definite structural position at $\partial I$). Each path has its own closed-form termination: A to B to A, C to A, D to a specific sub-path terminal. Thus in every case a lattice address exists. \qed
\end{proof}



% ===========================================================
\section{The Mathematical Rosetta Stone}\label{sec:rosetta}
% ===========================================================

Every mathematical expression maps to $P$--$D$--$T$ structure. This is not a metaphorical reading: the cardinality identifications $|P| = \Omega$, $|D| = n$, $|T| = [0/0]$ of Axiom~\ref{ax:cardinalities} are literal --- $P$ IS the mathematical infinite, $D$ IS the mathematical finite, $T$ IS the mathematical indeterminate. The universality anchor $\forall x: x \in \Sigma$ (Definition~\ref{def:Sigma}) guarantees that no mathematical object lies outside the framework, and the Subsumption Law (\S\ref{subsec:subsumption}) guarantees that every mathematical object decomposes into $P$-, $D$-, and $T$-components. This section makes the decomposition explicit for the principal domains of mathematics.

\subsection{Why mathematics works}\label{subsec:math-works}

Mathematics describes $P \circ D \circ T$ structure. Physics also describes $P \circ D \circ T$ structure (Axiom~\ref{ax:master}). Mathematics and physics therefore describe the same object --- and their agreement is structural, not coincidental. Wigner's observation of the ``unreasonable effectiveness of mathematics in the natural sciences'' (1960) receives a structural explanation: mathematics is effective because it IS the Descriptor language of $\Sigma$; physics is effective because it IS the substantiated face of $\Sigma$. Both are $P \circ D \circ T$ at different identification depths. When mathematicians do mathematics, they use $T$ (consciousness/agency) to navigate $D$-structured mathematical configurations grounded in $P$. Math and physics cannot fail to agree because they are readings of the same object.

\subsection{L'H\^opital's Rule as the $T$-navigation algorithm}\label{subsec:lhopital}

L'H\^opital's Rule~\cite{LHopital1696} is the canonical algorithm by which $T$ resolves indeterminate forms. When a limit process encounters $[0/0]$ or $[\infty/\infty]$ --- both signatures of $T$-engagement (\S\ref{subsubsec:two-indeterminate}) --- L'H\^opital's Rule prescribes: examine the Descriptor gradients (derivatives) of the numerator and denominator separately. Formally:
\[
\lim_{x \to a} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \left[\frac{0}{0}\right] \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \lim_{x \to a} \frac{f'(x)}{g'(x)} = L,
\]
provided the latter limit exists. In ET terms: $T$ at a choice point ($[0/0]$) cannot resolve the value directly; $T$ examines the $D$-gradients ($f' = \Delta D_{f}/\Delta P$, $g' = \Delta D_{g}/\Delta P$) to determine which configuration to substantiate. The resolution value $L$ is the outcome of $T$'s navigation through Descriptor space --- not a pre-existing value waiting to be found, but the result of the Traverser's act of engaging the Descriptor gradients at the approach point.

\paragraph{Worked example.} $\lim_{x \to 0} \sin(x)/x = [0/0]$. L'H\^opital: $\lim_{x \to 0} \cos(x)/1 = \cos(0)/1 = 1$. ET reading: the sine and $x$ both vanish at $x = 0$ ($D$-values approaching zero simultaneously), creating a $[0/0]$ choice point. $T$ examines the $D$-gradients: $\cos(x)$ (the rate of change of sine's $D$-value) approaches $1$ at $x = 0$ (maximum Descriptor alignment), and $1$ (the rate of change of $x$'s $D$-value) is constant. The ratio of gradients is $1$, and $T$ substantiates this value.

L'H\^opital's Rule is not limited to calculus: it is the general mechanism by which indeterminacy is resolved in the manifold. Wherever $T$ encounters a configuration with genuinely undetermined outcome, the resolution path proceeds through $D$-gradients --- the structural slopes of the Descriptor field at the approach point.

\subsection{Limits as traversal operations}\label{subsec:limits-traversal}

The mathematical limit $\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L$ is a Traverser operation: $T$ approaches configuration $a$ along the $P$-substrate, navigating the Descriptor field $f$, and substantiates the value $L$ at the approach point. The limit operation IS $T$-action --- which is why $T$'s cardinality is $[0/0]$, the canonical limit form. The Asymptotic Approach Theorem (Corollary~\ref{cor:asymptotic-approach}) is a direct instance: the lattice's descriptor gap $|\varepsilon|$ approaches zero as resolution $N$ increases, but never attains it --- $T$ navigating the lattice, substantiating ever-closer approximations without reaching the irrational target.

\subsection{Functions as Descriptor fields}\label{subsec:functions-PD}

A mathematical function $f: X \to Y$ is a Descriptor field over Points: the domain $X$ is $P$-content (the substrate of possible inputs), the codomain $Y$ is $D$-content (the constraint values), and the evaluation $f(x)$ is the specific Descriptor binding at Point $x$. The projection formula $\Pi_{N}$ of \S\ref{sec:lattice} is itself such a function: $r$ is the $P$-content, the lattice structure is the $D$-content, and the rounding is the $T$-act. Every function is a $(P \circ D)$ configuration, and function application is the binding operator $\circ$ at the level of mathematical structure.

\subsection{Derivatives as Descriptor gradients}\label{subsec:derivatives-DeltaD}

The derivative $f'(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} (f(x+h) - f(x))/h$ measures the rate of Descriptor change across Points: $\Delta D / \Delta P$. The limit form approaches $[0/0]$ as $h \to 0$ --- both numerator and denominator vanish simultaneously --- making every derivative a $T$-resolution of an indeterminate form (\S\ref{subsec:lhopital}). Derivatives appear throughout physics because they measure how $D$ changes across $P$: velocity ($\Delta D_{\text{position}} / \Delta D_{\text{time}}$), force ($\Delta D_{\text{momentum}} / \Delta D_{\text{time}}$), field gradient ($\Delta D_{\text{field}} / \Delta D_{\text{space}}$).

\subsection{Integrals as Traverser accumulation}\label{subsec:integrals-accumulation}

The integral $\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\,dx$ is $T$ accumulating Descriptor changes across a range of Points: the Traverser sweeps from $P$-position $a$ to $P$-position $b$, summing the $D$-values $f(x)$ encountered along the way. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus ($\int_{a}^{b} f'(x)\,dx = f(b) - f(a)$) connects local $T$-traversal (derivative) with global $T$-accumulation (integral): the total accumulated Descriptor change between two Points equals the difference of Descriptor values at those Points. The integration constant $C$ in indefinite integrals $\int f(x)\,dx = F(x) + C$ is a $T$-choice: the boundary condition that specifies which configuration to start from, undetermined until $T$ selects it.

\subsection{Continuity, differentiability, and discontinuity types}\label{subsec:continuity-types}

Continuity of $f$ at a point means the Descriptor field varies smoothly: $T$ can navigate through that point without encountering barriers. Differentiability means the Descriptor gradient exists: $T$ can measure the local slope at every point. The three classical types of discontinuity are manifold events:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item \emph{Jump discontinuity}: an abrupt $D$-switch at a Point --- a phase transition in the manifold, where the Descriptor value changes discretely.
\item \emph{Removable discontinuity}: a missing $D$-value at a single Point (a Descriptor gap in the sense of Principle~\ref{princ:descriptor-gap}), fillable by supplying the missing Descriptor.
\item \emph{Essential discontinuity}: multiple simultaneous $D$-values at a Point (a superposition state), where the Descriptor field oscillates without settling.
\end{itemize}
The $\partial I$ boundary (\S\ref{subsec:coherence-boundary}) is the extreme discontinuity: the point where the Descriptor field fails entirely and the configuration enters Incoherence.

\subsection{Complex numbers and the $D_{T}$ axis}\label{subsec:complex-DT}

The complex number $z = a + bi$ is a Point with two Descriptor dimensions: $a$ on the real axis ($D$'s operational domain, flat, $(R^{+},\times)$) and $b$ on the imaginary axis ($D_{T}$ --- the Descriptor content where $T$ directly operates, positively curved, $U(1)$). The imaginary axis is simultaneously a $D$-axis (it carries phase Descriptors, angular modes, rotational structure) and $T$'s operational domain (where $T$'s navigational capacity manifests as rotation). The identification $D_{T}$ captures both: it is $D$-content \emph{of and for} $T$.

The relation $i^{2} = -1$ is geometric necessity: two 90° rotations in the $D_{T}$ plane produce a 180° reversal (negation). The quartic cycle $i^{4} = 1$ (Remark~10.6) is the structural basis of the weak force ($d = 4$): the physical manifestation of the four-step $D \to T \to -D \to -T \to D$ cycle that $T$'s rotation generates in the Descriptor manifold.

\subsection{Mathematical operators as Traversers}\label{subsec:operators-T}

A mathematical operator ($d/dx$, $\int dx$, $\nabla$, $\Delta$) is a $T$-type entity: indeterminate until applied to a specific function, resolving to a value only upon engagement. An operator without its operand is in the $[0/0]$ state --- it has the capacity to act but no determinate output. Upon application, it produces a determinate result (a derivative value, an integral value, a gradient vector). The chain rule $\frac{d}{dx}[f(g(x))] = f'(g(x)) \cdot g'(x)$ is compound $T$-navigation: $T$ traversing two nested Descriptor fields simultaneously, with the product arising from the composition cost of two sequential navigations.

\subsection{Differential equations as manifold dynamics}\label{subsec:DEs-dynamics}

A differential equation is a relationship between Descriptor fields and their gradients --- the dynamics of $\Sigma$. An ODE describes a single $T$-worldline through Descriptor space; a PDE describes $T$-dynamics across extended $P$-substrate. The heat equation $\partial u / \partial t = \alpha \nabla^{2} u$ describes how thermal Descriptor values ($D_{\text{temperature}}$) propagate through spatial substrate ($P_{\text{space}}$) over $D$-time, with the diffusion coefficient $\alpha$ governing the rate of $D$-spread. The wave equation, Schr\"odinger equation, Einstein field equations, and Maxwell equations are all manifold dynamics equations describing how specific $D$-fields evolve across $P$-substrate under $T$-agency.

\subsection{The infinity hierarchy as $P$-structure}\label{subsec:infinity-hierarchy}

The transfinite hierarchy of set theory is $P$-structure at successive integrative levels. Countable infinity $\aleph_{0}$ is discrete structured $P$ (the integers as substrate positions). Uncountable infinity $2^{\aleph_{0}}$ is dense $P$-manifold (the reals as continuous substrate). Absolute Infinity $\Omega$ (the Cardinal $P$ itself) is all possible configurations, beyond every set-theoretic cardinal --- the full substrate. Each level in the hierarchy is a higher integrative reading of $P$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:int-resolution}), requiring finer lattice resolution to host.

\subsection{Probability as Unsubstantiated superposition}\label{subsec:probability-unsub}

A random variable is a Point in the Unsubstantiated state $\{P,D\}$ with multiple possible $D$-values awaiting $T$-engagement. The probability distribution is the weighting across possible Descriptor values. The expectation $E[X] = \sum x_{i} p_{i}$ is the weighted average over the Unsubstantiated $D$-superposition. Measurement is $T$-binding: the Traverser engages the $\{P,D\}$ superposition and substantiates a specific $D$-value, producing the Exception (a definite outcome). The variance $\mathrm{Var}(X)$ measures how spread the Unsubstantiated $D$-values are --- which connects directly to the manifold's base variance $V = 1/N = 1/12$ (Definition~\ref{def:variance}).

\subsection{The quantum mechanics mapping}\label{subsec:QM-mapping}

The quantum wavefunction $\psi(x,t)$ is a complex Descriptor field ($D$-content on $P_{\text{space}} \times D_{\text{time}}$) in the Unsubstantiated state: it describes what COULD be substantiated but has not yet been. Measurement is $T$-binding: a Traverser engages the wavefunction and substantiates one eigenvalue from the superposition. The Born rule ($P = |\psi|^{2}$) is the structural probability of each Descriptor value being the one $T$ selects. Wavefunction collapse is the transition $\{P,D\} \to \{P,D,T\} = E$ (\S\ref{subsec:decoherence-states}).

The structural parallel between $V = 1/12$ and $\hbar$ is that both are irreducible base quanta in their respective domains: $V$ is the minimum non-zero variance on the lattice (Definition~\ref{def:variance}); $\hbar$ is the minimum quantum of action in physics. The uncertainty principle $\Delta x \cdot \Delta p \ge \hbar/2$ maps to the lattice statement that minimum Descriptor-gap uncertainty at base resolution is $V = 1/12$. However, $V$ is dimensionless and $\hbar$ is dimensionful (units of action): $\hbar$ is the cosmological tower seed $R_{0}$ inherited from the parent tower, not derivable from the dimensionless framework alone.

\subsection{Matrix algebra as Descriptor transformations}\label{subsec:matrices-D}

A matrix is a Descriptor transformation operator: it maps one set of $D$-values to another. Rows are output Descriptors; columns are input Descriptors. Non-commutativity of matrices ($AB \neq BA$ in general) reflects the fact that the order of $D$-transformations matters in $\Sigma$ --- applying constraint $A$ before $B$ produces a different configuration than $B$ before $A$. Eigenvalues are the invariant $D$-scaling factors: the values that survive the transformation unchanged. Eigenvectors are the invariant $D$-axes: the directions in Descriptor space that the transformation preserves. The magical-impedance coupling strengths of Table~7.3 are the sublattice-family eigenvalues of the manifold's structural transformation operators.

\subsection{Topology as configuration boundaries}\label{subsec:topology-config}

The topological vocabulary generalises the manifold-state topology of Proposition~\ref{prop:topology}. An open set is a $D$-region whose boundary is not included --- $T$ can approach but not substantiate the boundary (the Incoherence structure: $I$ is open, $\partial I \cap I = \emptyset$). A closed set is a $D$-region whose boundary is included --- $T$ CAN substantiate the boundary (the Exception structure: $E$ is closed, $\partial E \subseteq E$). Compactness is finite $D$-range with all boundaries substantiable --- every sequence of $T$-navigations within the set has a convergent subsequence within the set.

\subsection{Set theory and the configuration power set}\label{subsec:sets-powerset}

The power set $2^{|D|}$ of a Descriptor set is the configuration space: the set of all possible $D$-combinations that can be applied to a given $P$-substrate. From $n$ Descriptors, $2^{n}$ configurations are possible. The doubling law of the LCM tower (Theorem~\ref{thm:doubling}: $\tau(N_{\ell}) = 6 \cdot 2^{\ell}$) is the structural consequence: each tower level adds one new sublattice family, doubling the number of available configurations.

\subsection{Symmetry and group theory at $N = 12$}\label{subsec:groups-N12}

The manifold symmetry $N = 12$ admits the group-theoretic decomposition $\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z} \cong \mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}$ (by the Chinese Remainder Theorem, since $\gcd(3,4) = 1$). The factor $\mathbb{Z}/3\mathbb{Z}$ reflects the three-primitive structure ($|\Pi| = 3$); the factor $\mathbb{Z}/4\mathbb{Z}$ reflects the four manifold states ($S = 4$). The unit group $(\mathbb{Z}/12\mathbb{Z})^{*} \cong V_{4}$ (the Klein four-group, Remark~10.6) has four elements $\{1, 5, 7, 11\}$ --- the four unit-residue generators of the palindromic cascade.

\subsection{Mathematical constants from ET}\label{subsec:constants-ET}

\paragraph{$e = 2.71828\ldots$} The natural base is the continuous $D$-propagation rate: the limit of compounded Descriptor growth as the compounding interval approaches zero. In the EML framework (\S\ref{subsec:eml}), $e = \mathrm{eml}(1,1)$ --- the terminal constant of the continuous-$D$ generator.

\paragraph{$\pi = 3.14159\ldots$} Half the period of $T$'s operational manifold $U(1)$ (\S\ref{sec:complex}). The full period is $2\pi$; $\pi$ is the half-rotation in the $D_{T}$ plane (\S\ref{subsec:complex-DT}). The ET-native derivation proceeds via the 12-gon: $\sin(\pi/12) = (\sqrt{6} - \sqrt{2})/4$, and $\pi$ is recovered as the limit of the Archimedean polygon sequence starting from the manifold's $N$-fold symmetry.

\paragraph{$\varphi = 1.61803\ldots$} The golden ratio is the quintic attractor at the $60$ET resolution level --- the root of $x^{2} - x - 1 = 0$, requiring the shadow family $d = 5$ (native at $N_{\ell} = 60$, the second tower level). The Fibonacci ratios $F(n+1)/F(n)$ converge to $\varphi$ as $n \to \infty$; the ratio $8/5 = F(6)/F(5) = 1.600$ is one convergent (not a replacement for $\varphi$). At $12$ET, $\varphi$ projects to $(k=8,\, d=3,\, \varepsilon = +33.09\text{¢})$; the quintic character emerges only at $60$ET+ where $d = 5$ is native.

