Gravitational binding, which selects minimum-variance configurations, systematically targets low-$d$ sublattices; the lattice structure is consistent with the empirical distribution of resonances.

\subsection{Biochemistry}\label{subsec:biology}

The step counts of major metabolic cycles are direct positive integers (denominator $=1$ step):
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item Citric acid (Krebs) cycle: $8$ steps. $\Pi_{12}(8) = (+36, 1, 0)$ --- exact $d=1$ octave class. The step count is $2^{3}$.
\item Urea cycle: $4$ core steps. $\Pi_{12}(4) = (+24, 1, 0)$ --- exact $d=1$.
\item Glycolysis: $10$ steps. $\Pi_{12}(10) = (+40, 3, -13.69)$ --- $d=3$ cubic.
\item ATP synthase rotational ring: typically $10$ $c$-subunits. Same projection as glycolysis.
\item Cell cycle: $4$ phases (G1, S, G2, M). $\Pi_{12}(4) = (+24, 1, 0)$.
\end{itemize}

The ``closure'' cycles (Krebs, urea) project to $d=1$ with $\eps=0$; their step counts are powers of $2$. The ``linear pathways'' (glycolysis, purine synthesis) project to $d=3$ with the major-third gap; their step counts are multiples of $2$ and $5$. The distinction between closure and linear pathway is reflected in distinct sublattice family assignments.

\subsection{Optical phase singularities}\label{subsec:optical-singularities}

Bucher et al.~\cite{Bucher2025} measure the ultrafast dynamics of optical phase singularity ensembles in phonon-polariton fields in hexagonal boron nitride (hBN), achieving deep sub-wavelength spatial ($20$\,nm $\approx \lambda_{\mathrm{PhP}}/30$) and deep sub-cycle temporal ($3$\,fs $\approx \tau/8$) resolution. The Identification Principle applied to wave propagation in the hyperbolic medium gives:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l l@{}}
\toprule
Primitive & Identification in the hBN phonon-polariton system\\
\midrule
$P$ & The spatial substrate of the crystal --- the hBN lattice\\
$D$ & The dispersion relation $\omega(\mathbf{k})$ --- the finite constraint governing wave propagation\\
$T$ & The wave field's phase --- the $U(1)$-valued quantity at each point; $T$-agency is the\\
    & topological dynamics (singularity creation, annihilation, $\pm 2\pi$ winding)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The data yield four lattice-addressable quantities.

\subsubsection*{Phase singularities as $\partial I$ boundary configurations}

Phase singularities are points of zero amplitude and undefined phase. At the singularity core, $D$-content (amplitude on $(\R^{+},\times)$) vanishes and $T$-content (phase on $(U(1),\times)$) is undefined, placing the configuration in the $\{P,T\}$ state (Incoherence, Definition~\ref{def:states}). The $\pm 2\pi$ winding of $T$ around the core exists in the neighbourhood, not at the core itself --- this is the Mediation Problem (Theorem~\ref{thm:mediation-problem}) made physical: $P$ and $T$ cannot bind without $D$'s finite structure. All six structural properties of $\partial I$ boundary configurations (\S\ref{sec:fractal}) are satisfied.

\subsubsection*{The $1/3$ velocity-tail fraction as $T$-weight}

The Berry--Dennis velocity distribution $P_{\pm}(|v|) = 8\langle v\rangle^{2}|v|/(8|v|^{2}+4\langle v\rangle^{2})^{2}$ has the property that exactly $1/3$ of singularities exceed the mean velocity $\langle v\rangle$. The substitution $w = 2u^{2}+1$ (with $u = |v|/\langle v\rangle$) maps the threshold $u=1$ to $w=3=|{P,D,T}|$; the integral from $w=3$ to $\infty$ versus $w=1$ to $\infty$ gives $1/3 = 1/|{P,D,T}|$, the $T$-weight. Below the mean: the $\{P,D\}$-constrained fraction $= K = 2/3$. Above the mean: the unconstrained $T$-fraction $= 1-K = 1/3$. The $1/3$ is the primitive count appearing in the velocity distribution of $T$-content objects.

\subsubsection*{Material classification via the Dimensionless Seed Ratio}

The natural reference period of a phonon-polariton material is $R_{0} = 1/\omega_{\mathrm{TO}}$ (one TO phonon oscillation period); the Dimensionless Seed Ratio is $r = \omega_{\mathrm{LO}}/\omega_{\mathrm{TO}}$. Different materials project to different lattice positions:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l c c c c@{}}
\toprule
Material & $\omega_{\mathrm{LO}}/\omega_{\mathrm{TO}}$ & $k$ & $d$ & $\varepsilon$ (cents)\\
\midrule
hBN upper band & $1610/1370 = 161/137$ & $3$ & $4$ & $-20.5$\\
hBN lower band & $830/780 = 83/78$ & $1$ & $12$ & $+7.6$\\
$\alpha$-MoO$_{3}$ [100] & $972/820$ & $3$ & $4$ & $-5.6$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
hBN upper and $\alpha$-MoO$_{3}$ both sit at $d=4$ (quartic) but at different descriptor gaps ($\varepsilon = -20.5$\,\textcent{} versus $-5.6$\,\textcent); hBN lower band sits at $d=12$ --- a different sublattice family entirely. The polariton properties of each material, including the phase-to-group velocity ratio, are classified by the Dimensionless Seed Ratio's lattice position. The measured $v_{\varphi}/v_{g} \approx 12 \pm 1$ for hBN is the $T/D$ propagation ratio at the $d=4$, $\varepsilon=-20.5$\,\textcent{} cell; $\alpha$-MoO$_{3}$ at the same $d$ but different $\varepsilon$ produces a different ratio.

\subsubsection*{Wavelength compression $\lambda/\lambda_{0} \approx 11$ and the $d=11$ harmonic family}

The polariton wavelength compression factor $\lambda/\lambda_{0} \approx 11$ (where $\lambda = 7\,\mu$m free-space and $\lambda_{0} \approx 630$\,nm polariton) identifies the $d=11$ undecimal harmonic family --- the last prime in $N_{\mathrm{FULL}} = \mathrm{lcm}(1,\ldots,11) = 27720$. Combined with the measured $v_{\varphi}/v_{g} \approx 12 = N$, the structural chain is:
\[
\lambda/\lambda_{0} \approx 11 \;\Rightarrow\; v_{\varphi} = c/11, \qquad v_{\varphi}/v_{g} = N = 12 \;\Rightarrow\; v_{g} = c/(11\times 12) = c/132.
\]
The integer $132 = \mathrm{lcm}(11,12) = n_{c}(d{=}11)$ is the canonical LCM-tower resolution at which the $d=11$ harmonic family first becomes native (Table~\ref{tab:twelve-harmonic-families}). The group velocity of hBN phonon-polaritons thus equals $c$ divided by the resolution at which the $d=11$ harmonic family first appears natively on the LCM tower. The system operates at the structural junction where the compression factor ($11$, the last prime of $N_{\mathrm{FULL}}$) and the velocity ratio ($12$, the manifold symmetry) combine to give $n_{c}(11) = 132$.

The lattice tower projection of the integer $11$ confirms the harmonic-family identification:
\begin{center}
